## Region / locality derivation from postcode

### `OwnerRegion` — city→region table, region-postcode ranges, locality + postcode validation
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/OwnerRegion.java`

Single source of truth. Tables: `Sydney→NSW (2000-2099)`, `Melbourne→VIC (3000-3099)`, `Brisbane→QLD (4000-4099)`; unknown city → `UNKNOWN`.
```java
public static String regionOf(String city) {
    return CITY_REGION.getOrDefault(city, UNKNOWN);
}
public static String localityOf(String city, String postcode) {
    String byPostcode = regionForPostcode(postcode);
    return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : regionOf(city);
}
private static String regionForPostcode(String postcode) {
    if (postcode == null || !postcode.matches("\\d{4}")) return null;
    int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
    for (Map.Entry<String, int[]> entry : REGION_POSTCODES.entrySet()) {
        int[] range = entry.getValue();
        if (value >= range[0] && value <= range[1]) return entry.getKey();
    }
    return null;
}
public static boolean isPostcodeValid(String city, String postcode) {
    if (postcode == null || !postcode.matches("\\d{4}")) return false;
    int[] range = REGION_POSTCODES.get(regionOf(city));
    if (range == null) return true;              // unknown region accepts any 4-digit
    int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
    return value >= range[0] && value <= range[1];
}
```
Note for the redesign: `localityOf` already prefers the postcode-derived region — this is exactly the `REGION` the new `customerCode` is meant to use. `regionOf`/`localityOf` are the obvious building blocks for the `<REGION>` segment.

- `locality` is surfaced on the DTO at read time by `OwnerMapper.locality` → `OwnerRegion.localityOf(city, postcode)`.
- `isPostcodeValid` is enforced at create time by `ValidateOwnerFields`.

